thanks tony and we're joined now by dr  tom engelsby  for advice on how voters can stay  healthy and safe as they cast their  ballots  dr inglesby is the director of the  center for health security at the  johns hopkins bloomberg school of public  health good morning doctor thanks for  being with us  how concerned anthony how concerned are  you about coronavirus transmission at  polling stations  and should people feel safe voting in  person  i think if people follow simple  simple instructions take normal kind of  sensible actions  that the risk can be as little as going  to the pharmacy or going to the grocery  store but they have to think about it  ahead of time  plan ahead of time and then act in ways  that public health suggests  when they're at the at the voting voting  place what about those polling stations  where masks are not required  yeah well in every place in the country  people should be using mass when they  vote i think i mean going backwards to  what they should be doing before they  even get there  people should think about when they can  vote where there's where the  the fewest number of people will be  there and in 20 states  today the polls are still open for early  voting so i think i'd recommend going  today as opposed tomorrow there's less  density of the of the crowds  if you go tomorrow then try to vote in a  time when  most people aren't there we know that  people go to the polls  and before work or during lunch or after  after work is over so if you can go  mid-morning mid-afternoon there are less  people there  before you go you should you bring your  pen  bring if you need to plan ahead bring a  chair if you need it  bring 60 hand sanitizer or hand  sanitizer with more  percent alcohol to be safe  and look at the the sample ballot before  you go  know who you're going to vote for know  what the ballot questions are so that  when you're inside  where the risks are higher than outside  you can get in and get out quickly  so that's what i do before you go but  when you're at the poll  then you follow the things that we're  supposed to be following all along wear  a mask whether or not your state  requires it it's important for everyone  it protects your neighbor and you  and the second thing you should do is  keep your distance  when you're in line make sure that you  stay six feet away or more from others  and it doesn't matter if people are  wearing masks you still need to stay  away from them by at least six feet  yeah and then before you go into the  polls or out of the polls make sure you  use  hand sanitizer yeah i should say in in  my polling station early voting last  week  everybody was being extremely careful  and everyone was being very respectful  of each other  it was very encouraging uh so hopefully  that's being replicated around the  country  as you've heard dr fauci has has said in  recent days that  that he fears we're in for a whole lot  of hurt in the coming weeks  uh and that we could not be positioned  more poorly  what do you fear is ahead of us at this  point  yeah unfortunately with the way that the  numbers are rising on a  daily and weekly basis i think we're  headed for very high numbers of cases  more hospitalizations and higher numbers  of people dying from this disease  i think it's it's not a foregone  conclusion i think we do still have our  fate in our hands  but governors and other leaders are  going to have to change direction in  places where cases are surging  and people are going to have to change  the way that they're acting and change  their decisions in places where p  where the cases are surging we're at 100  000 we're at 100  000 cases a day you think it can go a  lot higher than that  yeah there is no set peak for this virus  this virus  is looking for people to infect that's  what it does it hasn't changed since the  very beginning  and we know that most of the country  still is susceptible to this virus it's  still vulnerable to this virus  so the thing that's going to change it  isn't the virus but it's what we decide  to do  it's whether we decide to wear masks  whether we decide to distance ourselves  to not gather in large numbers  we have to do those things ourselves to  change the course dr tom inglesby thank  you so much for being with us
 

